  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CST, skies are becoming partly sunny across central South \nDakota, but remain cloudy/overcast across northeast South Dakota \ninto west central Minnesota. TROWAL-forced rain bands continue to \npersist and rotate south through the region, mainly centered down \nthe I-29 corridor and Prairie Coteau, on the backside of the \nlarge/broad occluded upper low churning over Minnesota. Winds are \nout of the northwest 15 to 25 mph with gusts up to 35 mph and \ntemperatures were mainly in the 40s to low 50s.\n\nAfter this last rainband over the eastern/southeastern forecast \nzones departs the CWA, the forecast is dry for the entire 7-day \nforecast. Upper level ridging dominates the steering flow winds \naloft for the majority of the forecast period. Surface high pressure \nbuilds over region tonight, resulting in some fog potential, \nparticularly over portions of the CWA that clear off tonight. Short \nterm guidance is developing another area of low clouds over southern \nCanada and North Dakota/northern Minnesota tonight and bringing it \nsouthward into the CWA mainly between 08Z and 15Z Thursday. Unsure \nwhat will happen to any areas of fog (probably dissipate) trying to \ndevelop, if/once low clouds spread over it late tonight/early \nThursday morning.\n\nTemperatures will remain near normal through Friday. But the low \nlevel thermal advection pattern is forecast to transition to a WAA \npattern Friday afternoon/evening which persists through the weekend. \nWarmer (above normal) temperatures then look to hold through the \nmiddle of next week. Wind speeds and afternoon relative humidity \nvalues appear to behave themselves on the fire weather forecast side \nof things, as well, for the time being. Not a whole lot to right \nhome about just now in the forecast.",
